% not a single macro, so that a later \AddOsg...Processor can extend
% \SetOsg...Processor (which resets the sequence to one entry, so
% today's replace-the-single-processor behavior is unchanged) without
% breaking the storage format. Applying the sequence folds it left to
% right: each processor wraps the previous one's output, so the
% first-added processor sees the original content and the
% last-added one sees the result of all earlier processors.
